{"name":"Evil Eye","hex":"#1100cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"evil eye","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wicked look conveying dislike or envy that in many cultures is believed to be able to cause bad luck or injury; also, the ability to cause bad luck or injury through such a look, supposed to be possessed by some people.","The charm used to ward off the evil eye; a nazar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/evil_eye"}],"entries":[{"title":"Evil eye","blocks":[{"text":"The evil eye is a supernatural belief in a curse brought about by a malevolent glare, usually inspired by envy. Amulets to protect against it have been found dating to around 5,000 years ago. It is found in many cultures in the Mediterranean region, the Balkans, Eastern Europe, West Asia, Central Asia, South Asia, Africa, the Caribbean, and Latin America, with such cultures often believing that receiving the evil eye will cause misfortune or injury, while others believe it to be a kind of supernatural force that casts or reflects a malevolent gaze back upon those who wish harm upon others. The idea also appears multiple times in Jewish rabbinic literature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Kapadokya%27dan_Nazar_Boncu%C4%9Fu.jpg/330px-Kapadokya%27dan_Nazar_Boncu%C4%9Fu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evil_eye","wikidata":"Q1020115"},{"title":"Evil","blocks":[{"text":"Evil, as a concept, is usually defined as profound immorality, and it is related to acts that cause pain and suffering to others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Demon._A_miniature_from_the_Georgian_manuscript_of_the_12th_century.jpg/330px-Demon._A_miniature_from_the_Georgian_manuscript_of_the_12th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evil","wikidata":"Q15292"},{"title":"Eye","blocks":[{"text":"An eye is a sensory organ that allows an organism to perceive visual information. It detects light and converts it into electro-chemical impulses in neurons (neurones). It is part of an organism's visual system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Krilleyekils.jpg/330px-Krilleyekils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye","wikidata":"Q7364"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Roman_golden_ring_with_an_evil_eye_protective_talisman%2C_Vinkovci_Municipal_Museum_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Roman_golden_ring_with_an_evil_eye_protective_talisman%2C_Vinkovci_Municipal_Museum_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Roman_golden_ring_with_an_evil_eye_protective_talisman%2C_Vinkovci_Municipal_Museum_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Roman_golden_ring_with_an_evil_eye_protective_talisman%2C_Vinkovci_Municipal_Museum_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Colonia Aurelia Cibalae 3rd century ring with an evil eye talisman. City Museum of Vinkovci, Croatia[8]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoman_golden_ring_with_an_evil_eye_protective_talisman%2C_Vinkovci_Municipal_Museum_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/HorusEye.png/500px-HorusEye.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/HorusEye.png/500px-HorusEye.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eye of Horus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHorusEye.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Eye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg/500px-Eye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Eye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg/1280px-Eye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Eye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg/500px-Eye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kylix eye cup (530–520 BC), inscribed with Chalcidian text. It features an eye motif, likely to ward off the evil eye.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEye-cup_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_589.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Hotamis_Kilim.jpg/500px-Hotamis_Kilim.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Hotamis_Kilim.jpg/500px-Hotamis_Kilim.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail of a 19th-century Anatolian kilim, with rows of crosses (Turkish: Haç) and scattered S-shaped hooks (Turkish: Çengel), both to ward off the evil eye[31]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHotamis_Kilim.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Votive_tree_cappadocia.jpg/500px-Votive_tree_cappadocia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Votive_tree_cappadocia.jpg/1280px-Votive_tree_cappadocia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Votive_tree_cappadocia.jpg/500px-Votive_tree_cappadocia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tree with nazars in Cappadocia, Turkey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVotive_tree_cappadocia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/John_Phillip_-_The_Evil_Eye_%281859%29.jpg/500px-John_Phillip_-_The_Evil_Eye_%281859%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/John_Phillip_-_The_Evil_Eye_%281859%29.jpg/500px-John_Phillip_-_The_Evil_Eye_%281859%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John Phillip, The Evil Eye (1859), a self-portrait depicting the artist sketching a Spanish Romani woman who thinks she is being given the evil eye.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Phillip_-_The_Evil_Eye_(1859).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/J%C3%BCdisches_Halsgezeig.jpg/500px-J%C3%BCdisches_Halsgezeig.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/J%C3%BCdisches_Halsgezeig.jpg/500px-J%C3%BCdisches_Halsgezeig.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pink protective neckband with a heart-shaped pendant and coins designed to distract the evil eye, 1944, Basel. In the Jewish Museum of Switzerland's collection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJ%C3%BCdisches_Halsgezeig.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/One_of_the_masterpieces_of_the_Kurd%27s_Heritage_Museum%2C_Sulaymaniyah%2C_Iraqi_Kurdistan._A_tiny_lead_scroll_inscribed_with_a_Hebrew_text%2C_for_prayer_and_warding_off_evils.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/One_of_the_masterpieces_of_the_Kurd%27s_Heritage_Museum%2C_Sulaymaniyah%2C_Iraqi_Kurdistan._A_tiny_lead_scroll_inscribed_with_a_Hebrew_text%2C_for_prayer_and_warding_off_evils.jpg/1280px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/One_of_the_masterpieces_of_the_Kurd%27s_Heritage_Museum%2C_Sulaymaniyah%2C_Iraqi_Kurdistan._A_tiny_lead_scroll_inscribed_with_a_Hebrew_text%2C_for_prayer_and_warding_off_evils.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tiny lead scroll about 200 years old inscribed with a Hebrew text for prayer and warding off evils. The scroll was inserted into some form of silver accessory (Kurdish: لوله و زه نجير, lit. 'bar and chain'), wrapped with a piece of cotton, to be hung on the chest. Kurd's Heritage Museum, Sulaymaniyah, Iraq.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOne_of_the_masterpieces_of_the_Kurd's_Heritage_Museum%2C_Sulaymaniyah%2C_Iraqi_Kurdistan._A_tiny_lead_scroll_inscribed_with_a_Hebrew_text%2C_for_prayer_and_warding_off_evils.jpg"}],"sounds":{"evil eye":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-evil_eye.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/24/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-evil_eye.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-evil_eye.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/24/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-evil_eye.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-evil_eye.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[]}}